        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "8a6c57e6-063c-5fc7-a556-d5df90ba221e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Reno–Tahoe International Airport, Washoe County, Nevada, Reno, Lake Tahoe, Fort Reno, Oklahoma, Las Vegas\n- People: Jesse L. Reno, Frank A. Keating\n- Organizations: United States Army\n- Roles: Union General, Governor of the U.S. Zone of Germany, Chief U.S. Military Advisor to Korea\n- Events: Mexican–American War, Utah War, American Civil War, Battle of South Mountain, World War II\n\nFacts:\n- Jesse Lee Reno was born on April 20, 1823, and he became a career United States Army officer.\n- Jesse L. Reno served in the Mexican–American War, the Utah War, on the western frontier, and as a Union General during the American Civil War.\n- Jesse L. Reno died on September 14, 1862, at the Battle of South Mountain in the American Civil War.\n- Fort Reno in Oklahoma is named for General Jesse L. Reno.\n- Frank Augustus Keating was born on February 4, 1895, and he became a career officer of the United States Army.\n- During World War II, Frank A. Keating commanded the 102nd Infantry Division.\n- Frank A. Keating later served as Governor of the U.S. Zone of Germany and as Chief U.S. Military Advisor to Korea.\n- Frank A. Keating died on April 28, 1973.\n- Reno–Tahoe International Airport is a public and military use airport located three nautical miles southeast of downtown Reno in Washoe County, Nevada.\n- Reno–Tahoe International Airport has the codes IATA RNO, ICAO KRNO, and FAA LID RNO.\n- Reno–Tahoe International Airport is Nevada's second busiest commercial airport after McCarran International Airport in Las Vegas.\n- Reno–Tahoe International Airport is named after Jesse L. Reno and Lake Tahoe.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
